    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Macroeconomics examines economy-wide output, prices, employment, and financial relationships. For Iran, interpreting these measures requires particular care over reporting periods, exchange-rate conventions, and the distinction between historical observations and forecasts.",
    "tags": ["Macroeconomics", "National accounts", "GDP", "Exchange rates", "Economic statistics"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:52:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:52:02Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Understanding Iran's Macroeconomy | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "A guide to interpreting Iranian output statistics, reporting calendars, exchange-rate conventions, and economic forecasts."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "national-accounts",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Start with the measurement",
        "content": "National accounts organize information about production, income, and expenditure into a consistent framework. Gross domestic product, or GDP, is a central aggregate within that framework. The World Bank's metadata describes production, expenditure, and income as alternative approaches to measuring it. These approaches help distinguish a change in total economic activity from a change in one particular industry. [s1]\n\nComparability is a practical problem. Services can be difficult to measure, informal activities require estimation, and rebasing can change the weights used in national accounts. A historical series may consequently be revised even when the underlying period is long past. Keeping the dataset code and release context is part of interpreting the number, rather than merely an administrative detail. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "currency-conventions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Currency conventions change comparisons",
        "content": "A dollar-denominated GDP figure depends on a conversion convention. The IMF's May 2023 regional statistical appendix provides a concrete Iranian example: its historical nominal GDP calculations used the official exchange rate through 2017 and the NIMA exchange rate from 2018. The appendix explains that staff considered the latter more representative of transaction values. This is a documented convention for that publication, not a rule that can be assumed for every Iranian dataset. [s2]\n\nPurchasing power parity, or PPP, answers a different question. The World Bank's International Comparison Program compares prices and expenditure to account for differences in purchasing power across economies. Its methodology requires comparable national-account definitions and coverage. A PPP comparison is therefore not interchangeable with converting output at a financial exchange rate. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "release-discipline",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keep the release and period attached",
        "content": "The World Bank's Spring/Summer 2023 Iran Economic Monitor illustrates why a table must be read with its column labels. Its selected-indicators table separates actual results, estimates, and projections, and reports Iranian years using paired labels such as 2022/23. A projection does not become an observed result simply because its reporting period has subsequently ended. [s4]\n\nThe IMF likewise explains that historical WEO estimates are revised as country teams receive updated information from national authorities. Comparisons should therefore identify both the period being measured and the publication supplying the value. Combining historical numbers from different releases without checking revisions can create a series that no institution actually published. [s5]\n\nThe next step is to read output alongside prices, employment, productivity, and distribution. The linked pages separate those questions so that a GDP total does not have to stand in for every aspect of economic performance. Those companion indicators still require dedicated Iranian source research before this overview can offer a complete account of their relationships."
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
