    "description": "Gross domestic product measures production within an economy over a reporting period. Iran's nominal GDP in current U.S. dollars is useful as a published monetary series, but changes in it must not be interpreted directly as changes in real output or household welfare.",
    "tags": ["GDP", "National accounts", "Nominal GDP", "Economic measurement", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:52:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:52:02Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran's Gross Domestic Product: Meaning and Annual Data | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Inspect Iran's published nominal GDP series and understand why current-dollar values differ from real economic growth."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "definition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What GDP counts",
        "content": "Gross domestic product measures the value of final goods and services produced within an economy during a period. The production approach adds value created at successive stages, avoiding the repeated counting of intermediate inputs. Income and expenditure approaches examine the same production through the incomes it generates and the final purchases associated with it. [s1]\n\nThe distinction between nominal and real GDP is essential. Nominal GDP values production at current prices. Real GDP adjusts for price changes to examine changes in output. Neither measure, on its own, establishes how income is distributed or whether environmental damage has accompanied production. GDP is an important economic aggregate, but it is not a complete measure of well-being. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "published-series",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The annual series presented here",
        "content": "The dataset below reproduces World Bank current-dollar GDP observations distributed through the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis's FRED service. The inspected series identifies its source as World Development Indicators, its frequency as annual, and its units as current U.S. dollars. Its displayed update predates the editorial cutoff. The requested default window is represented from 2012 through 2026; the unfinished final year is missing. [s2]\n\nThe chart shows substantial variation in the dollar valuation of Iranian production. It does not isolate changes in production volumes. No exchange-rate conversion has been performed here, and no growth rate has been calculated from these nominal values. The precision retained reflects the published download, rather than a claim that national output is measured with comparable economic certainty. [s2]\n\nFRED labels annual observations with January dates. Those labels are retained as year identifiers, without treating them as verified boundaries of Iran's underlying national-account periods. Resolving that calendar mapping and documenting the World Bank's Iran-specific currency conversion are necessary before stronger comparative conclusions can be drawn. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "nominal-gdp-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: published nominal GDP in current U.S. dollars",
          "dataset_id": "nominal-gdp-usd",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["gdp-current-usd"],
          "x_axis_label": "Published annual year label",
          "y_axis_label": "Current U.S. dollars, unscaled",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The published current-dollar value falls between 2012 and 2015, rises in 2016–2017, falls through 2020, rises through 2024, and falls in 2025. These movements describe nominal dollar valuation, not real output growth. No value is available here for 2026. [s2]"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "nominal-gdp-table",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Published annual GDP observations and the missing final year",
          "dataset_id": "nominal-gdp-usd"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "international-comparisons",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Choosing a comparison",
        "content": "Purchasing-power comparisons address differences in price levels across economies. The World Bank's International Comparison Program uses prices and national-account expenditure information to construct purchasing power parities. Its framework distinguishes consumption, capital formation, and net exports, while emphasizing comparable definitions and coverage. A PPP estimate and a current-dollar GDP total therefore answer different questions. [s3]\n\nFor this page, the defensible conclusion is limited: an inspected annual nominal series is available, but it does not establish Iran's real growth path. A fuller account requires a comparable constant-price series, an explicit treatment of reporting calendars, and documentation of revisions. Until those checks are complete, the page remains a research draft.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
